%description:

Tcp.receiveBufferMax:free -- the receive buffer grows above 262144 bytes.

The claim (Tcp.ned): "windowAutoTuning: the largest receive buffer that auto-tuning reaches, by
Dynamic Right-Sizing, out-of-order data or the overflow of its memory".

The classes: free (32MiB, the default, the value of Linux tcp_rmem[2]: not reached) and capped
(a value that the growth reaches). The code: TcpConnection::growRcvBuffer() and the growth for
the overflow of the buffer limit the buffer to the value. This test separates "free" from "capped".

Host under test: host2, with window auto-tuning and a receive buffer of 131072 bytes
(preconditions), whose application reads at once; host1 sends 20000000 bytes; the round trip
is 40 ms. Observation: level 3, at 0.7 s, the receive buffer of host2 is above 262144 bytes (about
1330000).

%# parameter: Tcp.receiveBufferMax
%# class: free
%# value: 32MiB
%# key: *.host2.tcp.receiveBufferMax
%# expected-result: PASS

%includes:
#include "ProtocolTest.h"

%global:

using namespace inet;
using namespace inet::protocoltest;

Define_ProtocolTestProgram()
{
    return ProtocolTest("TcpReceiveBufferMaxFree")
        .act(action().at(0.7)
                 .describe("the receive buffer of host2 at 0.7 s")
                 .assertModule("host2.tcp.conn-*", "state.rcvBufferSize > 262144"));
}

%activity:
EV << "TcpReceiveBufferMaxFree compiled\n";

%inifile: omnetpp.ini

[General]
*.host2.tcp.receiveBufferMax = 32MiB    # the parameter under test
*.host2.tcp.windowAutoTuning = true
*.host2.tcp.receiveBufferSize = 131072B
*.linkDelay = 10ms
*.host1.app[0].sendBytes = 20000000B
sim-time-limit = 0.8s
include ../../parameter/ini/_bulk.ini
include ../../parameter/ini/_base.ini

%contains: stdout
PROTOCOLTEST TcpReceiveBufferMaxFree: PASS
